        # ---------------------------------------------------------------- your people
        dict(kind='wide', section='Your people', title='Your people: some answer fewer routine messages, some approve, and someone owns the agent',
             visual=dict(kind='l10_people'),
             caption='Jumia cut about 7% of its staff with AI; Capitec added 711 while it scaled AI.',
             notes='Welcome back. We turn to your people, because an agent changes jobs before it changes results. Three roles feel it. A service agent answers fewer routine messages and spends more time on sensitive cases and vulnerable customers, as at Octopus; the skill to grow is spotting when the agent is wrong. An approver types less and reads more, and fears being blamed for what the machine wrote. And a new role appears: the agent owner, who sets the keys, reads the log and knows the off switch. The numbers point both ways. Jumia cut about 7 per cent of its staff as it moved work to AI, while Capitec, the South African bank, added 711 staff as it scaled AI. What do your people expect?'),
        dict(kind='idea', title='Supervising agents is a new management job: write down who does what',
             visual=dict(kind='l10_owner'),
             lead='One named owner answers for every agent.',
             points=['The sponsor sets the level of freedom.', 'IT holds the keys and the off switch.', 'Staff report oddities without blame.'],
             notes='Whatever they expect, supervising agents is now a management job, and it needs names. The leader who sponsors the agent sets its level of freedom. IT grants the keys and runs the off switch. One named owner answers for the results and reads the log. An approver says yes or no to sends, payments and credits. And every member of staff can report anything odd, without blame. On the right is a lesson in speaking for an agent. In May 2021 Lemonade, an American insurer, said online that its AI picked up non-verbal clues in claim videos. After a public backlash it deleted the posts and said AI never denies a claim on its own. Say exactly what your agent does.'),
        dict(kind='stat', title='A warning before you buy: Gartner forecasts that over 40% of agent projects will be cancelled',
             big='40%', text='of agentic AI projects cancelled by the end of 2027, forecast by Gartner in June 2025: rising costs, unclear value, weak risk controls.',
             waffle_label='more than 40 in every 100',
             source='Reuters via The Star, 25 June 2025. It is a forecast.',
             notes='Before you sign anything, here is a sobering forecast. In June 2025 the research firm Gartner forecast that more than 40 per cent of agentic AI projects will be cancelled by the end of 2027, because of rising costs, unclear business value or weak risk controls. It also estimated that of the thousands of vendors claiming to sell agents, only about 130 offer the real thing; it called the rest agent washing, old products with a new label. It is a forecast, reported by Reuters, and nobody has checked it yet. But its three causes are exactly what today’s controls and next session’s business case address, and they lead straight to the questions you should ask.'),
        dict(kind='table', title='Questions to ask your team and your vendor before an agent acts for you',
             headers=['Ask', 'Why it matters'],
             rows=[['Which keys does it hold: read, write, send, spend?', 'The keys set the worst case'],
                   ['What can it do without a person approving?', 'Anything that cannot be undone must wait'],
                   ['Who owns it, and who reads the log?', 'A team often means nobody'],
                   ['How fast can we switch it off?', 'Minutes, by removing its access'],
                   ['What does it do with instructions inside emails or files?', 'Hidden instructions turn reading into acting'],
                   ['Is it a real agent, and what will it cost to run?', 'Gartner expects many projects to be cancelled']],
             widths=[6.4, 5.4], icons=['lock', 'person', 'people', 'switch', 'flag', 'calculator'],
             notes='Everything so far comes down to six questions, one per row. Ask which keys it holds, read, write, send or spend, because the keys set the worst case. Ask what it can do without a person approving, because anything that cannot be undone must wait. Ask who owns it and who reads the log. Ask how fast you can switch it off; the answer should be minutes. Ask what it does with instructions found inside emails or files, because hidden instructions turn reading into acting. And ask whether it is a real agent and what it will cost to run, since Gartner expects so many projects to be cancelled. Now we turn them on your own portfolio.'),
